WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.405 --> 00:00:02.130
How do we know the translation is correct?

2
00:00:02.130 --> 00:00:06.823
Whether the machine-executable
version, the rulework, is correct?

3
00:00:06.823 --> 00:00:08.327
Part of that...

4
00:00:10.009 --> 00:00:13.711
lies in making laws and
regulations testable.

5
00:00:13.711 --> 00:00:18.895
We convert a rulework with a

6
00:00:18.895 --> 00:00:24.179
language model into a
machine-executable version:

7
00:00:24.179 --> 00:00:29.162
the statutory text is
turned into these actions.

8
00:00:29.162 --> 00:00:34.713
On the other side, an explanatory
memorandum is turned into a

9
00:00:34.713 --> 00:00:38.456
scenario, a test for the law.

10
00:00:39.489 --> 00:00:44.772
We convert the law into a rulework, a

11
00:00:44.772 --> 00:00:50.595
case into a test, and then we run it.

12
00:00:50.595 --> 00:00:53.857
With a whole lot of such tests

13
00:00:53.857 --> 00:00:58.119
that capture the intent of the law,

14
00:00:58.119 --> 00:01:03.302
the desired behaviour,

15
00:01:03.302 --> 00:01:07.845
we convince ourselves that the law at least
works on these scenarios the way those

16
00:01:09.233 --> 00:01:10.696
That is one answer to the question.

17
00:01:10.696 --> 00:01:16.187
Another answer: as a legal analyst
or legislative lawyer, you are

18
00:01:16.187 --> 00:01:20.892
ultimately responsible

19
00:01:20.892 --> 00:01:21.332
for how the law works.

20
00:01:21.332 --> 00:01:26.336
You must be able to look at this
and see whether it is desired.

21
00:01:26.336 --> 00:01:30.819
We are making sure that legal
analysts and legislative lawyers

22
00:01:30.819 --> 00:01:32.621
do not have to look at this.

23
00:01:32.621 --> 00:01:38.005
This may look too nerdy, so
we are building a better

24
00:01:38.005 --> 00:01:43.248
user interface, so that a
lawyer can look at it and

25
00:01:43.248 --> 00:01:44.970
say with conviction:

26
00:01:45.905 --> 00:01:49.600
the interpretation of the law is correct.

27
00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:55.322
Already now we do not know whether the

28
00:01:55.322 --> 00:01:58.429
translation of legislation
into software is correct.

29
00:01:58.429 --> 00:02:02.570
Software often executes
legislation, and it is often not

30
00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:06.471
transparent about how exactly that
works. Yet we use that software.

31
00:02:06.471 --> 00:02:10.312
That is no excuse for letting
translations be wrong.

32
00:02:10.312 --> 00:02:11.952
They have to be right.

33
00:02:11.952 --> 00:02:13.613
It only shows that we

34
00:02:15.795 --> 00:02:20.117
already have a lot of trust
in the translations by

35
00:02:20.117 --> 00:02:21.538
software engineers.

36
00:02:21.538 --> 00:02:27.360
We propose taking that away from
software engineers and publishing it

37
00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:29.401
openly, so that everyone looks at it.
